76:  * <h3>Thread safety</h3>
77:  * <p>
78:  * Registry objects are assumed to be thread safe.
79:  * No external synchronization is performed around registry access.
80:  * As registry objects may be used across multiple requests, they should be thread safe.
81:  * <p>
82:  * Registries that are <b>created</b> per request however do not need to be thread safe.
83:  *
84:  * <h3>Ordering</h3>
85:  * <p>
86:  * Registry objects are returned in the reverse order that they were added (i.e. Last-In-First-Out).
87:  *
88:  * <pre class="java">{@code
89:  * import com.google.common.base.Joiner;
90:  * import ratpack.registry.Registry;
91:  *
92:  * import static org.junit.Assert.assertEquals;
93:  *
94:  * public class Example {
95:  *   public static void main(String... args) throws Exception {
96:  *     Registry registry = Registry.registry(r -> r
97:  *         .add("Ratpack")
98:  *         .add("foo")
99:  *         .add("bar")
100:  *     );
101:  *
102:  *     assertEquals("bar", registry.get(String.class));
103:  *
104:  *     String joined = Joiner.on(", ").join(registry.getAll(String.class));
105:  *     assertEquals("bar, foo, Ratpack", joined);
106:  *   }
107:  * }
108:  * }</pre>
109:  * <p>
110:  * While this is strictly the case for the core registry implementations in Ratpack, adapted implementations (e.g. Guice, Spring etc.) may have more nuanced ordering semantics.
111:  * To the greatest extent possible, registry implementations should strive to honor LIFO ordering.
112:  */
113: public interface Registry {
114: 
115:   /**
116:    * Provides an object of the specified type, or throws an exception if no object of that type is available.
117:    *
118:    * @param type The type of the object to provide
119:    * @param <O> The type of the object to provide
120:    * @return An object of the specified type
121:    * @throws NotInRegistryException If no object of this type can be returned
122:    */
123:   default <O> O get(Class<O> type) throws NotInRegistryException {
124:     return get(TypeToken.of(type));
125:   }
126: 
127:   /**
128:    * Provides an object of the specified type, or throws an exception if no object of that type is available.
129:    *
130:    * @param type The type of the object to provide
131:    * @param <O> The type of the object to provide
132:    * @return An object of the specified type
133:    * @throws NotInRegistryException If no object of this type can be returned
134:    */
