46: /**
47:  * An extension module that provides support for Coda Hale's Metrics.
48:  * <p>
49:  * To use it one has to register the module and enable the required functionality by chaining the various configuration
50:  * options.  For example, to enable the capturing and reporting of metrics to {@link ratpack.codahale.metrics.CodaHaleMetricsModule.Config#jmx(ratpack.func.Action)}
51:  * one would write: (Groovy DSL)
52:  * </p>
53:  * <pre class="groovy-ratpack-dsl">
54:  * import ratpack.codahale.metrics.CodaHaleMetricsModule
55:  * import static ratpack.groovy.Groovy.ratpack
56:  *
57:  * ratpack {
58:  *   bindings {
59:  *     add new CodaHaleMetricsModule(), { it.jmx() }
60:  *   }
61:  * }
62:  * </pre>
63:  * <p>
64:  * To enable the capturing and reporting of metrics to JMX and the console, one would
65:  * write: (Groovy DSL)
66:  * </p>
67:  * <pre class="groovy-ratpack-dsl">
68:  * import ratpack.codahale.metrics.CodaHaleMetricsModule
69:  * import static ratpack.groovy.Groovy.ratpack
70:  *
71:  * ratpack {
72:  *   bindings {
73:  *     add new CodaHaleMetricsModule(), { it.jmx().console() }
74:  *   }
75:  * }
76:  * </pre>
77:  *
78:  * <h2>External Configuration</h2>
79:  * The module can also be configured via external configuration using the
80:  * <a href="http://www.ratpack.io/manual/current/api/ratpack/config/ConfigData.html" target="_blank">ratpack-config</a> extension.
81:  * For example, to enable the capturing and reporting of metrics to jmx via an external property file which can be overridden with
82:  * system properties one would write: (Groovy DSL)
83:  *
84:  * <pre class="groovy-ratpack-dsl">
85:  * import com.google.common.collect.ImmutableMap
86:  * import ratpack.codahale.metrics.CodaHaleMetricsModule
87:  * import ratpack.config.ConfigData
88:  * import static ratpack.groovy.Groovy.ratpack
89:  *
90:  * ratpack {
91:  *   bindings {
92:  *     ConfigData configData = ConfigData.of()
93:  *       .props(ImmutableMap.of("metrics.jmx.enabled", "true")) // for demo purposes we are using a map to easily see the properties being set
94:  *       .sysProps()
95:  *       .build()
96:  *
97:  *     addConfig(new CodaHaleMetricsModule(), configData.get("/metrics", CodaHaleMetricsModule.Config))
98:  *   }
99:  * }
100:  * </pre>
101:  *
102:  * <h2>Metric Collection</h2>
103:  * <p>
104:  * By default {@link com.codahale.metrics.Timer} metrics are collected for all requests received and {@link Counter} metrics for response codes.
105:  * The module adds a {@link RequestTimingHandler} to the handler chain <b>before</b> any user handlers.  This means that response times do not
106:  * take any framework overhead into account and purely the amount of time spent in handlers.  It is important that the module is registered first
107:  * in the modules list to ensure that <b>all</b> handlers are included in the metric.
108:  * </p>
109:  * <p>
110:  * Additional custom metrics can be registered with the provided {@link MetricRegistry} instance
111:  * </p>
112:  * <p>
113:  * Example custom metrics: (Groovy DSL)
114:  * </p>
115:  * <pre class="groovy-ratpack-dsl">
116:  * import ratpack.codahale.metrics.CodaHaleMetricsModule
117:  * import com.codahale.metrics.MetricRegistry
118:  * import static ratpack.groovy.Groovy.ratpack
119:  *
120:  * ratpack {
121:  *   bindings {
122:  *     add new CodaHaleMetricsModule(), { it.jmx() }
123:  *   }
124:  *
125:  *   handlers { MetricRegistry metricRegistry -&gt;
126:  *     handler {
127:  *       metricRegistry.meter("my custom meter").mark()
128:  *       render ""
129:  *     }
130:  *   }
131:  * }
132:  * </pre>
133:  * <p>
134:  * Custom metrics can also be added via the Metrics annotations ({@link Metered}, {@link Timed} and {@link com.codahale.metrics.annotation.Gauge})
135:  * to any Guice injected classes.
136:  * </p>
137:  *
138:  * @see <a href="http://metrics.codahale.com/" target="_blank">Coda Hale's Metrics</a>
139:  */
